s Hong Kong zombie horror movie style. <Subject 1> Master Nine casts Taoist spells at the altar in <Subject 2> ancestral hall. He places a yellow paper crane on a white porcelain bowl filled with glutinous rice, forms standard clear Taoist finger mudras to channel magic power. A sudden pop sound bursts bright magical orange flame out of the bowl, accompanied by thick billowing gray-white smoke. The enchanted paper crane is lifted by mystical power and slowly floats up to circle mid-air. Vintage film grain texture, old-school practical in-camera fire and smoke special effects, no character dialogue throughout the video.\nretention_analysis\n<Subject 1>: fully_preserved, completely retain Master Nine’s appearance, yellow Bagua Taoist robe and solemn mudra-casting posture from <Picture 1>\n<Subject 2>: fully_preserved, fully keep all altar furnishings, dim candle-lit ancient environment from <Picture 2>\n<Subject 3>: fully_preserved, all props including rice bowl, glutinous rice, yellow paper crane, joss sticks, candles and magical smoke are fully retained\n<Picture 1>: fully_preserved, character face and costume design remain unchanged all the time\n<Picture 2>: fully_preserved, ancestral hall layout, retro lighting and mottled wall texture are completely inherited\ndetailed_description\nOverall art style: 1990s Hong Kong zombie horror film aesthetic, heavy 35mm vintage film grain, low-key warm candle primary lighting, hard light and shadow contrast, subtle vignetting on frame edges; old practical fire & smoke effects, no exaggerated modern glowing particles, strictly restore original visual effect of vintage Hong Kong in-camera composite shooting.\n[Shot 1] 00:00-00:02 Opening ritual, static medium shot\nFull medium shot showing the complete ancestral hall altar, fully follow the scene layout of <Picture 2>. Red candle flames flicker constantly, thin incense smoke drifts upward slowly. <Subject 1> Master Nine stands solemnly in front of the altar, wearing bright yellow Bagua Taoist robe. He raises both hands to his chest and forms highly recognizable standard Taoist mudra: index and middle fingers straight and closed together, the other three fingers curled tightly inward, palms facing down with distinct knuckles. Candlelight cuts sharp alternating light and shadow across his face, robe fabric sways slightly under indoor breeze, thin incense smoke drifts horizontally across the frame.\n[Shot 2] 00:02-00:04 Place paper crane, slow push-in close-up\nCamera slides forward evenly to close-up shot focusing on altar table surface, a white porcelain bowl full of white glutinous rice sits in frame center. Master Nine holds a yellow origami paper crane between thumb and forefinger, gently lays it flat on top of rice. His other hand maintains complete mudra hovering right above the rice bowl with fixed hand shape, his eyes locked on the bowl while muttering silent incantations, candle light reflection clearly shows on smooth porcelain surface.\n[Shot 3] 00:04-00:06 Sudden flame burst and billowing smoke, medium close shot\nMaster Nine presses his mudra hand slightly down toward the bowl to pour mystical power. With a short crisp pop sound, bright orange magical flame surges straight upward from inside the bowl. Flame is confined strictly within the porcelain bowl without burning surrounding objects. Thick heavy gray-white smoke billows out instantly after flame bursts, wrapping the whole bowl and half covering the priest’s casting hand. Glutinous rice grains glow under firelight, the paper crane bounces slightly upward impacted by energy shock. Slight natural camera shake and enhanced film grain to restore original vintage movie fire texture.\n[Shot 4] 00:06-00:08 Paper crane levitate and fly, upward follow shot\nFlame inside rice bowl fades gradually, only faint glowing embers left. Yellow paper crane unfolds its paper wings automatically, lifts off rice surface and floats upward steadily held by Taoist magic, flapping paper wings gently in small movements. Camera tilts upward slowly following the crane’s ascending track, a thin faint golden smoke trail drags behind the crane as it circles slowly in candle-lit hall space. Master Nine keeps full mudra gesture the whole time, stretches his arm slightly toward the crane, eyes firmly tracking the flying paper crane without deviation.\n[Shot 5] 00:08-00:10 Final freeze frame, pull-back medium shot\nCamera pulls backward evenly back to medium framing. Master Nine still stands before altar holding fixed mudra posture, yellow paper crane hovers mid-air and spins slowly. Red candles keep flickering, incense smoke fills dim ancient ancestral hall, subtle vintage vignetting adds on frame edges, the video freezes at last frame.\noverall_soundscape\nOpening segment: quiet ambient hum of ancestral hall, tiny crackling sound of burning joss sticks, soft rustle of Taoist robe fabric;\nPaper crane placement: faint crinkling sound of paper, low muffled murmur of silent incantation;\nFlame burst segment: sharp sudden pop sound, whooshing flow of flame, swirling airflow of thick smoke, tiny granular rustle of vibrating rice grains;\nCrane flying segment: faint low hum of mystical energy, soft air rustle from flapping paper wings;\nClosing freeze frame: continuous crackle of candle flame, spacious distant quiet reverb of old large hall.\nnon_diegetic_music\nAuthentic retro Hong Kong folk horror soundtrack, deep solemn suona, wooden fish tapping matched with restrained traditional drum set. Melody rises slightly when bowl flame bursts, calms down smoothly after paper crane takes flight, creating mysterious solemn Taoist ritual atmosphere."], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "value"}, "name": "value", "label": "value", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "值"}], "flags": {}, "type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline", "title": "提示词", "mode": 0, "bgcolor": "#353", "size": [370, 390], "pos": [-1175.2496337890625, 6136.412109375], "id": 138, "properties": {"widget_ue_connectable": {"